## The Numbers Matter

With 807 total recalls this year, the food industry has faced its share of scrutiny, and deservedly so. Out of these, a staggering 234 have been Class I recalls--a clear sign of serious risks to consumer health. In total, there have been 1165 recalls to date, underscoring the ongoing challenge of ensuring food safety.

## D'Dioses Dominates Headlines

This week, D'Dioses Fruit Pops, Inc. has taken the dubious honor of being the major offender, with 11 recalls reported on July 22, 2026. The issue? A variety of their ice pops have been found to potentially contain undeclared milk, pecans, pistachios, yellow #5, and red #40. These missing allergen declarations result in Class I recalls, the most severe type.

Their products under scrutiny include flavors like Mango, Nuez, Tamarindo, Fresa Leche, Arroz con Leche, and others. With UPCs such as 827912008548 and 710594511836, these items are now under recall, leaving consumers wondering how such a glaring oversight occurred.

## Other Notable Cases

Beyond D'Dioses, Nutricia North America finds itself in hot water due to subpotent levels of Vitamin A in their Liquid Hope Organic Whole Foods Meal Replacement Peptide Formula Berry Medley. Mislabeling and insufficient testing standards are to blame here, resulting in a Class II recall.

Meanwhile, Fayus Inc. also joins the list with a recall of their OLA-OLA Pounded Groundnuts (Intwilo) for undeclared peanuts, another fundamental allergen oversight that lands them a Class II classification.

## The Verdict

This current recall spree highlights a concerning trend: preventable issues like undeclared allergens and mislabeling continue to plague the industry, demonstrating a lack of due diligence in quality control processes.

D'Dioses Fruit Pops serves as a striking case study in how to not handle food safety, with a whopping 11 Class I recalls this month alone. When allergens like milk, nuts, and food dyes are missed in labeling, lives are at risk.

The takeaway? Brands, especially repeat offenders, must step up and assure consumers that their products meet safety standards. If not, as history shows, the consequences could be dire for both public health and brand reputation.
